ata abstraction layer  now a data abstraction layer is a fairly  common design pattern  in in software engineering and it also  takes the guys of something like a  middleware  in the case of sharechat they were  already using libraries internally to  kind of obfuscate dynamodb  and provide that functionality to the  application so we wanted to take  advantage of that  the traditional approach would have had  the data abstraction layer live  inside of the service so literally  inside the code  but because we were moving to kubernetes  we had the opportunity  of using a sidecar approach to make the  same thing happen  and this gave us a few really key  benefits  that we would have otherwise not been  able to get this modified sidecar  approach effectively  took the shape of a database driver that  ran as a sidecar process  and allowed us to do things like  connection pooling for the pod  we were also able to do a single driver  for multiple different kinds of  languages and just have a different  library that would interact with it over  grpc and this  obviously gave us a dramatic performance  increase in terms of the  the kind of code that we were running  because we wrote everything in goal  and it also allows us to in the future  potentially change  the database redirections and this  pattern works so well that we're  actually going to open source this with  google as well  the critical piece over here though was  that sharechat didn't need to change  anything inside their application layer  at all they just need to swap out the  library that was being used to access  the database and this gave us  an enormous benefit in terms of the  speed with which  we could actually make this migration  occur  so i'm going to talk about that  migration in a little bit more detail  this was obviously a fairly gargantuan  task as you saw but the earlier slides  that venkates showed you  um we were talking about a substantial  number of both tables  as well as data and in order for us to  be able to move this over from aws to  google  in a repeatable and reliable manner we  had to use  a multi-stage process so the three  phases that we ended up with  effectively looked like this we had a  starting  with a batch of an upload of all of the  data  until a specific point in time and at  the moment that we actually  started that dump of data from dynamodb  we  also started inserting all mutations  that occurred to the dynamodb tables  into pub sub we were going to use that  in t1  which was basically after we were done  with the load we wanted to be able to  play back all the changes that happened  to the database  after we had loaded that point in time  snapshot  following that and this is the the most  crucial phase  when we were actually sort of in sync we  wanted to be able to start streaming  data  directly from aws to google and uh this  was probably one of the most complicated  parts of the entire system because we  couldn't use pubsub to do this we had to  build our own  system to enable that kind of streaming  from dynamodb  directly into both cloud bigtable and  cloud spanner  so let's talk about the batch processing  so we took  exports of the table data from dynamodb  which ended up inside s3  and we would basically watch and consume  those and stuff them into an sqs queue  that would be processed by ec2 instances  that would both uh compress and transfer  that over to gcs  on the google side we were watching and  consuming those buckets  through cloud functions that were  triggering uh individual  dags inside of airflow which was running  on cloud composer  that would run on top of a data flow  multiple jobs to both  uh load data into cloud spanner as well  as cloud bigtable simultaneously  and this obviously was dependent upon  the configurations that we had as to  which table  went into which system on the basis of  where the indexes were  so all of the tables that had multiple  indexes would end up in cloud spanner  and all the tables that had single  indexes would end up in cloud bigtable  and give us better performance the load  process itself  was also extremely complicated um as you  can imagine  firstly we're trying to take data that's  inside a nosql system and put it inside  a sql system in the case of cloud  spanner  that required us to actually add missing  columns when we  detected them inside the data that we  were getting  because a lot of this was legacy data  there weren't very  stable schemas and taking advantage of  nosql allows you to effectively create  keys and values  as you see fit well that information  wasn't present  when we dumped the data um in some kind  of format that would allow us to create  the tables  um before we did the transfer so we had  to actually do that on the fly  so the code that we wrote inside  dataflow would actually process  all of those individual rows and add the  appropriate tables  column families and missing columns as  required  we also had a dead letter pattern that  dealt with rejected records and allowed  us to retry them after we had made the  appropriate changes to either the data  type  or the schema itself  once we were done with that initial  transfer we had to switch to doing the  stream processing  this is where it started getting really  interesting so in order to make this  happen  we used a system on dynamodb called  dynamodb streams  now what that does is it effectively  puts all the mutations that are  happening inside dynamodb  into a stream that can be consumed and  it retains the data for about 24 hours  the lambda functions that get triggered  on the back of that had to be configured  because it got really expensive really  fast  so we set those up so that they would  take batches of 10 000 records  in five minute intervals and push those  over to some custom code that we wrote  on the google side running on compute  engine written in golang  that would allow us to both push the  data in real time  into cloud spanner and cloud bigtable  depending upon the configurations that  we had in place  and this is really what allowed us to do  this um  we tried to use uh pub sub and and there  were multiple  concerns that we ran into with that some  of them were latest  latency related and some of them were  related to ordering of the records in  order to make sure that it was going  into the database systems at the right  time  as you can imagine latency and  performance is absolutely key over here  and we actually built a whole bunch of  interesting code to be able to manage  any kind of breaks that might occur in  the stream itself  so after we were done with that transfer  it fell to uh  our team to make sure that venkatesh was  happy with the outcome and that meant  making sure that the data  actually matched born both systems and  that's where this comes in  bank addiction thanks michelle  performance and audit  are two understated steps in any  migration process  they never get the due diligence and i  want to talk a little bit about  what we did to end up focusing on these  two aspects  so you always start with data that's  publicly available right it's great to  have  data which is a starting point but you  make sure that you verify them  um so what we did was to set up a dummy  setup in our stating environment so in  this example that i have for you  we had a 40 node cluster for a real  table called tag posts  which was set up on spanner which had  about 160  gb of data uh with about five indexes  and we  hammered it um with a redirect ratio of  a four is to one  this allowed us to see how this banner  table  actually scaled with the amount of  traffic that it was getting and what was  its breaking point  and once you had that the next key part  and the most important part  is to try the same in production and the  reason for that is  your physical setup for staging and  production are going to be fundamentally  different  and you do this and you rinse and repeat  for most of the key flows and the key  tables  that you would expect to hit a lot of  traffic during normal and during peak  times and this would give you  a lot of comfort when you turn on the  migration step  completely onto one side  the two different audit parts that are  important for any migration are  for one for streaming and the other  which we will talk about in the next  slide  for streaming it's extremely important  for us to make sure  that the data has been caught up from  the source  and the system of truth to the new  system that is under test in this case  aws was the source of truth and google  cloud would be the system under test  so the graph that we have in front of  you was uh data that we got from influx  which we were ending up inserting into  and visualizing grafana  so you see that initially at time t  equal to zero you have  about 18 to 19 hours of lag and  ultimately it takes about six hours for  the data to actually catch up  and get close down to zero as much as  possible again  you do this for your key metrics for  your key tables and  these are extremely important to have at  your disposal  to make sure that your business metrics  are doing as well as  your system metrics now looking at  audit for data consistency the way we  did this  was to make sure that we replayed the  events that were happening on aws  on google cloud and did a simple json  diff of the two response objects that we  got back  and if there were any differences we  would end up logging that so the values  that you see here  are real values that we got when we  initially started out our migration  processes  and this allowed us to make sure that we  we fix this we take care of this we  handle this  before we shift even the first user over  and that gave us the confidence to go  extremely confidently to business and  say you know what  the system is as good as as the business  metrics and we should not see  any perceptible impact on users as they  end up moving from one system to the  other  and ultimately i want to focus a little  bit on learnings but before that  i want to start off with a small story  uh which started at around  the first week of july not too long ago  uh where we launched  our short video app called much um which  took just about 30 hours  from ideation to the actual launch um  and guess what we were all ready um  google cloud was ready for us in terms  of scale in terms of the kind of  traffic that we were expecting around  the same time  because of geopolitical reasons our  traffic on the sharechat app  also um went astronomical we went to  almost 3x 4x the amount of data that we  were  typically used to and we didn't even  blink an eye we were able to  horizontally scale  with zero to minimal code changes um  close enough about a week from then  we made the decision to move um all of  the traffic for mulch which was  quite a bit onto singapore and all this  really happened behind the scenes  seamlessly thanks to the amazing support  from google that we ended up receiving  care for the minimal changes that we had  to do on the dns  entries we really didn't have much in  terms of challenges that we faced during  that  shift over and ultimately you know it's  it's kudos to everybody who has been  involved  in this and i wanted to use this as the  poster child for the benefits that we  have gotten  uh just three months after the migration  process  and ultimately the learnings from the  migration itself um i think it's  important to know that  the migration or any migration of this  nature is not going to be a linear path  you could plan for all you want you  could build all the contingencies in  place you would still end up  encountering things  of the unknown nature and you just have  to fight your way through it  but what's very key and important is the  top-down ownership  and the commitment that you need to have  to make sure to get through all these  challenges that have come your way  and sign up for you know long weekends  and nights that will  ultimately um be the outcome um  solve your db choices first extremely  important right i've mentioned this  before i'm going to keep mentioning this  your data is going to be the ultimate  decider of how good your migration  process is your data  doesn't get moved correctly you could  have any number of challenges  even with all the computes in place even  with all the scalability inputs  and then ultimately separate out your  migration and your optimization tasks a  lot of the people who want to end up  doing one step of the migration process  then ending up putting an optimization  step on top of that  it doesn't work very well in fact it's  it's a it's chaotic it's extremely  disastrous um we didn't do it and we  would never do it even if you have to  redo this  all over again there's a lot of  learnings but the key part is separate  them out  make sure that you focus on the  migration if you have cost concerns  talk to google talk to your cloud  provider i'm sure they'll have a path  forward for you guys  and ultimately work as a single team  folks i can't emphasize this enough we  had  a lot of different parties associated  with this we had  developers we had the google pso team we  had actual google engineers from spanner  from big table  from from core engineering teams we had  um  our set of consultants we had cloud  cover who was our partner in crime  and we worked as a single unit which  means that if i had feedback to give i  would not just give it to my folks i  would give it to everybody  um so finally i want to thank all these  people involved  um an amazing set of  aligned work amazingly focused set of  people that were involved in this  process  it would be impossible to end up  thanking everybody that made this  possible  but i still want to give out my  heartfelt thanks from both me and vishal  for making this a reality thank you  folks thank you for everything  you
 

